    "id": "prop:buffered-coupling",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{2,\\mathrm{loc}}\\), \\(n>4r\\), \\(i\\in I_n\\), and \\(w\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), the restriction coupling satisfies \\(Z_{n,i}^{(w)}=Z_i^{(w)}\\) almost surely, and \\(|\\Lambda_n\\setminus I_n|/|\\Lambda_n|=2r/n\\to0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(P\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M_{2,\\mathrm{loc}}\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(r\\)",
      "\\(I_n\\)",
      "\\(Z_{n,i}^{(1)}\\)",
      "\\(Z_{n,i}^{(2)}\\)",
      "\\(Z_i^{(1)}\\)",
      "\\(Z_i^{(2)}\\)",
      "\\(\\Lambda_n\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:two-wave-local-class",
      "def:buffered-restriction"
    ],
    "justification": "The exact interior coupling makes one buffered F\\o lner sequence the sole finite-to-infinite-volume regime.",
    "gap": "BaryshnikovYukich2005 studies stabilizing finite-volume scores, while this proposition records the boundary deletion for two repeated exposure experiments on a fixed local graph.",
    "consumer": "An online experimenter can implement the diagnostic after dropping a prespecified fixed-width endpoint buffer.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{2,\\mathrm{loc}}\\), \\(n>4r\\), \\(i\\in I_n\\), and \\(w\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).  Couple the finite-volume assignment to the restriction \\(D^{(w)}|_{\\Lambda_n}\\) of the infinite assignment.  Since \\(i\\in I_n\\),\n\\[\n [i-r,i+r]\\cap\\mathbb Z\\subseteq\\Lambda_n.\n\\]\nBy the radius-\\(r\\) score-locality condition, the exposure and outcome entering the score at \\(i\\) depend only on \\((U_j,D_j^{(w)})\\) in this interval.  By the graph edge-range condition, restriction deletes no graph neighbor used there.  Moreover, conditional on \\(\\mathcal F_U\\), the assignments in this interval have the same product \\(\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\) law in the finite and infinite constructions.  Hence the conditional exposure probabilities agree, so every factor in the IPW formula agrees.  Thus \\(Z_{n,i}^{(w)}=Z_i^{(w)}\\) almost surely.\n\nThe complement of \\(I_n=\\{r+1,\\ldots,n-r\\}\\) in \\(\\Lambda_n\\) consists of the \\(r\\) sites at each endpoint.  Consequently\n\\[\n \\frac{|\\Lambda_n\\setminus I_n|}{|\\Lambda_n|}=\\frac{2r}{n}\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(r\\) is fixed.",
